{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>My lord asked his servants</b> I do not know the reason for the length of Judah's words in recounting what had already taken place between them. And what the Rabbis said (Genesis Rabbah 93:6) — \"Is this what you call 'setting an eye' when you said you would set your eye upon him?\" — is not a valid argument, for a ruler who commands that a man be brought before him does not do so on condition of exempting him from the evils he may commit, all the more so in the case of stealing the goblet from the king's house, from which he drinks. And at the outset he had already set his eye upon him for good and called him peace with the words \"may Elohim be gracious to you, my son\" (Genesis 43:29), and he prepared a banquet for all of them in his honor before him in the royal palace, and he gave them abundant provisions, and he gave them grain as much as they could carry, more than the silver they had brought him, as I explained (Ramban on Genesis 44:1) — so what was there to do to him?\n\nAnd what appears to me on the level of peshat is that these are nothing but supplications to stir his compassion, for Judah thought that this man was a fearer of Elohim, as he had told him, and as he had conducted himself toward them with compassion — like one who fears sin — to comfort them over the distress he had caused them (Genesis 43:23). And this is the substance of the narrative: he said to him, \"We under duress told you about this our brother because of my lord's questioning, and we also did not consent to bring him down before you according to your first command, but only said that the youth cannot leave his father; yet we undertook to bring him because of the fury of famine, for you said we would not see your face again. Our father refused to listen until we were all in danger of returning to procure a little food, and then he consented in fear and anxiety. And now, seeing that the youth would die of a bitter soul, therefore let your servant's plea fall before you to have compassion on us and on the old man, and take me instead of the youth as a permanent slave, for I am better than him, and it shall be a charitable deed for you.\" This is the sense of the entire passage.\n\nAnd it is possible that \"and let your servants bring down the gray hairs of your servant our father\" is an honorific plural: \"and you shall bring down the gray hairs of your servant our father,\" and similarly \"and you shall sin against your people\" (Exodus 5:16).\n\nAnd one may say further, according to the way of our Rabbis who said, \"this is indeed the setting of an eye\": he says, \"for you are like Pharaoh\" — you ought to stand by your word and your interpretation, for it was at your bidding that we brought him down under great duress, as he will recount. But he refrained from spelling it out explicitly to him; yet there is hidden in his words an implication that the affair of the goblet was a scheme contrived by him to frame them. For why would he seek to see him against their will? And so they said in Genesis Rabbah (93:8): Judah said to him, \"Know that you have come upon us with a fabrication! How many provinces came down to buy food — did you question any of them the way you questioned us? Perhaps it is your daughter we seek, or your sister you seek!\" They say that this was hinted at in his words."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {0 "The closing quotation from Genesis Rabbah 93:8 is rendered literally; the midrashic thrust implies that Joseph's interrogation was suspiciously personal, suggesting an ulterior motive."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:44:49.177987271Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}